CAT = "iiqeGoodFaith"; SEG = "UGF"
Q = [
("Insurable interest is best defined as a person's:",
 "Legal relationship to the subject matter", ["Possession of the property insured", "Interest earned on premiums paid", "Wish to make a profit from a loss"],
 "Insurable interest is a legally recognised relationship that gives the right to insure. Possession alone is not enough, so a thief cannot insure stolen goods."),
("An insurance agreement made without insurable interest is:",
 "Void", ["Voidable by the insured", "Valid but unenforceable", "Valid if premium is paid"],
 "Without insurable interest the agreement is void. Whether premiums are returned depends on the illegality rules, the IO and the Marine Insurance Ordinance."),
("A creditor wishes to insure property owned by a debtor that is not mortgaged to him. The creditor:",
 "Has no insurable interest in it", ["Has full insurable interest", "Can insure up to the debt", "Needs only the debtor's consent"],
 "A financial relationship alone is not enough. A creditor has an insurable interest in the debtor's life but not in unmortgaged property."),
("A parent may insure the life of a child or ward who is:",
 "Under 18 years of age", ["Under 21 years of age", "Living at home at any age", "Financially dependent at any age"],
 "One may insure the life of a child or ward under 18, and the policy does not become invalid when the life insured turns 18."),
("A dry cleaner holding customers' clothes may insure them because it is a:",
 "Bailee", ["Mortgagee", "Trustee", "Executor"],
 "A bailee takes possession of goods with the owner's consent but without ownership passing, and has an insurable interest in the goods bailed."),
("A landlord buys cover for lost rent if fire makes the building unusable. His insurable interest is in a:",
 "Legal right to future income", ["Liability to his tenants", "Life of the tenant", "Bailment of the tenants' goods"],
 "Anyone in a position of potential loss through loss of future income or infringement of rights, such as rent, may insure that legal right."),
("A woman insured her husband's life, then divorced him before he died. For her claim, when did she need insurable interest?",
 "When the policy began", ["When he died", "Both at the start and at death", "At each policy anniversary"],
 "For life insurance the interest is needed only at inception. A former spouse who insured her husband's life while married can still claim."),
("Under a cargo policy on goods at sea, the owner must hold insurable interest:",
 "At the time of loss", ["At inception only", "At inception and at loss", "When premium is first paid"],
 "Marine insurance requires interest at the time of loss; the same rule probably applies to other contracts of indemnity."),
("A policyholder assigns only the right to the policy proceeds to a friend. The assignee:",
 "Needs no insurable interest", ["Must have insurable interest", "Needs the insurer's consent", "Becomes the new insured"],
 "An assignment of the right to insurance money needs no interest on the assignee's part and no insurer consent; it may even take effect as a gift."),
("Which policies can usually be assigned without the insurer's consent?",
 "Life and marine cargo policies", ["Motor and fire policies", "Liability and household policies", "Medical and travel policies"],
 "In practice life policies and marine cargo policies are assignable without consent; other policies usually require it."),
("The principle of utmost good faith requires the proposer to:",
 "Disclose all material facts unasked", ["Answer only the questions asked", "Disclose only facts in writing", "Pay premium before cover starts"],
 "Under utmost good faith each party must reveal material facts whether or not asked, so a fire proposer must reveal past losses even with no question on them."),
("A material fact is one that would influence the judgement of:",
 "A prudent insurer", ["The particular insurer", "A reasonable insured", "The insurance agent"],
 "The test is a prudent insurer fixing the premium or deciding whether to accept the risk, not the actual insurer or the insured's view."),
("For a personal accident proposal, the fact that the proposer works as a salesperson mainly affects:",
 "The premium only", ["Acceptance only", "Both acceptance and premium", "Neither decision"],
 "Some material facts go only to acceptance, some only to premium, and some to both. Occupation here affects the rate rather than acceptance."),
("Without being asked, a commercial fire proposer need NOT disclose that:",
 "The premises have automatic sprinklers", ["The premises had two fires last year", "Flammable goods are stored inside", "A previous insurer declined cover"],
 "Facts that diminish the risk need not be disclosed in the absence of a question, along with common knowledge and facts the insurer knows."),
("A medical policy starts on 1 June. Before that date, but after acceptance, the insured learns he has malaria. At common law he:",
 "Need not disclose it", ["Must disclose it at once", "Must disclose it at the first claim", "Must cancel the policy"],
 "At common law the duty ends when the contract is concluded. The insurer would rely instead on a pre-existing condition exclusion."),
("At common law the duty of utmost good faith revives:",
 "When a general policy is renewed", ["At each life policy anniversary", "At the start of every month of cover", "Only when a claim is made"],
 "The duty revives at renewal of a non-life policy and when alterations are requested, but not on a life policy's anniversary."),
("A fire policyholder asks mid-term to add theft cover. He must disclose material facts about:",
 "The theft risk being added", ["Nothing, as the policy is running", "Only his fire claims history", "Only facts the insurer asks about"],
 "When an alteration is requested, the duty applies to the change, so security and past thefts must be disclosed."),
("An applicant innocently states the wrong age of his house, and the error is material. This is:",
 "Non-fraudulent misrepresentation", ["Fraudulent misrepresentation", "Non-fraudulent non-disclosure", "Fraudulent non-disclosure"],
 "Giving false information is misrepresentation; failing to give information is non-disclosure. An honest or careless error is non-fraudulent."),
("An insurer discovers a non-fraudulent breach of utmost good faith. Its remedy at common law is to:",
 "Avoid the contract from inception", ["Refuse only the current claim", "Keep the policy and the premium", "Cancel from the date of discovery"],
 "The aggrieved party may avoid the whole contract from inception, returning premiums for a non-fraudulent breach, or waive the breach."),
("The Code of Conduct for Insurers says an insurer should not deny a claim because of:",
 "Innocent misrepresentation", ["Fraudulent non-disclosure", "A fraudulent claim", "A breach that caused the loss"],
 "Under the Code, claims should not be denied for innocent misrepresentation (except marine and aviation) or for a non-fraudulent breach of warranty that did not cause the loss."),
]
